  "articleBody": "Taping and mudding requires steady hand technique and patience. Rushing the process leads to visible joints and bubbling tape that ruin your final paint job. Here are critical mistakes to watch for:\n\n- Leaving air pockets beneath joint tape: Trapped air prevents joint tape from bonding to the sheetrock, causing blisters that peel later.\n- Recoating damp compound too quickly: Premixed mud often requires up to 24 hours to cure. Adding new mud over damp layers traps moisture and delays drying.\n- Applying heavy, unfeathered edges: Thick ridges are difficult to sand flat. Mud must be wiped down with wider knives to feather seamlessly.\n- Sanding too aggressively through paper tape: Over-sanding cuts into tape fibers and weakens joint strength, requiring re-taping.\n- Evaluating the wall surface before applying primer: Raw mud absorbs light differently than drywall paper; primer is essential to check for true flatness.\n\nWhen Professional Guidance Helps\nExtensive butt joints, corner beads, and large open living areas with high lighting show every minor flaw. HH Remodeling LLC delivers dependable taping, mudding, and interior finish work across Denver and Aurora. Call Abraham Becerra at 303-505-0866 to review your interior finishing plans.",
